1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,233
Ten seconds to atmosphere.

2
00:00:02,593 --> 00:00:06,622
Velocity, one hundred and seventy thousand
kilometres an hour.

3
00:00:06,982 --> 00:00:11,102
There's no ground down there, nothing to
land on, just down.

4
00:00:11,812 --> 00:00:13,617
This is Mara Voss.

5
00:00:13,977 --> 00:00:16,764
She doesn't exist, and neither does her
capsule.

6
00:00:17,124 --> 00:00:21,388
Nothing we know how to build would survive
what happens next.

7
00:00:21,748 --> 00:00:26,742
But every number you're about to hear is
real, measured by the machines that went

8
00:00:26,742 --> 00:00:27,420
before her.

9
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:30,636
One of them got this far in 1995.

10
00:00:30,996 --> 00:00:34,307
It lasted fifty-eight minutes before the
pressure silenced it.

11
00:00:34,667 --> 00:00:36,483
Mara is going to keep going.

12
00:00:36,843 --> 00:00:38,145
All the way to the centre.

13
00:00:38,855 --> 00:00:40,144
Here we go.

14
00:00:41,644 --> 00:00:44,143
Contact, contact, everything's shaking!

15
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:47,481
The window, the window is on fire!

16
00:00:48,191 --> 00:00:52,625
At this speed, the gas in front of the
capsule can't get out of the way.

17
00:00:52,985 --> 00:00:57,229
It piles up into a shock wave and turns
into glowing plasma.

18
00:00:57,589 --> 00:00:59,305
Sixteen thousand degrees.

19
00:00:59,665 --> 00:01:02,880
Almost three times hotter than the surface
of the Sun.

20
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,639
Then the braking hits.

21
00:01:04,999 --> 00:01:09,742
For a few seconds, Mara weighs two hundred
and twenty-eight times her normal weight.

22
00:01:10,102 --> 00:01:12,309
More than fifteen tonnes.

23
00:01:12,669 --> 00:01:14,578
No human could survive that.

24
00:01:14,938 --> 00:01:16,361
The real probe did.

25
00:01:16,721 --> 00:01:17,878
It was built to.

26
00:01:18,588 --> 00:01:19,781
I'm still here.

27
00:01:20,141 --> 00:01:21,525
I think I blacked out.

28
00:01:21,885 --> 00:01:23,152
The parachute is open.

29
00:01:24,652 --> 00:01:28,340
I'm at the cloud tops, and it's minus one
hundred and sixty outside.

30
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:29,820
Everything is white.

31
00:01:30,530 --> 00:01:35,683
She's falling through the top of Jupiter's
weather: three layers of cloud, stacked

32
00:01:35,683 --> 00:01:37,819
about seventy-one kilometres deep.

33
00:01:38,179 --> 00:01:39,991
Ammonia ice on top.

34
00:01:40,351 --> 00:01:42,687
Ammonium hydrosulfide below it.

35
00:01:43,047 --> 00:01:44,830
And at the bottom, water.

36
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:49,266
Something's flashing beneath me, it's
lightning, and it's enormous.

37
00:01:49,976 --> 00:01:52,209
Jupiter's water clouds make lightning.

38
00:01:52,569 --> 00:01:57,454
And Juno spotted something even stranger:
hail made of ammonia and water slush,

39
00:01:57,454 --> 00:01:59,062
falling out of the storms.

40
00:01:59,422 --> 00:02:01,833
Scientists call them mushballs.

41
00:02:02,543 --> 00:02:04,395
Wind just hit us sideways.

42
00:02:04,755 --> 00:02:07,964
I'm reading six hundred and forty metres
per second.

43
00:02:08,674 --> 00:02:10,388
That's what the real probe measured.

44
00:02:10,748 --> 00:02:13,595
Almost twice the speed of sound on Earth.

45
00:02:14,305 --> 00:02:16,497
It's getting darker, and warmer.

46
00:02:17,997 --> 00:02:22,699
About an hour after entry, Mara reaches a
very special place.

47
00:02:23,059 --> 00:02:28,081
In 1995, NASA's Galileo probe stopped
transmitting right here.

48
00:02:28,441 --> 00:02:30,658
About a hundred and eighty kilometres
down.

49
00:02:31,018 --> 00:02:33,814
Twenty-two times the pressure of Earth's
air.

50
00:02:34,174 --> 00:02:38,002
Around a hundred and fifty degrees, hotter
than boiling water.

51
00:02:38,362 --> 00:02:40,771
Its last signal came from this depth.

52
00:02:41,131 --> 00:02:42,658
Then, silence.

53
00:02:43,368 --> 00:02:45,727
Passing the depth where Galileo went
silent.

54
00:02:46,087 --> 00:02:48,528
There's nothing here, just dark.

55
00:02:48,888 --> 00:02:53,679
From now on, nobody has ever seen this,
and I'm the first person to see it.

56
00:02:54,389 --> 00:02:57,780
Below this point, everything we know comes
from the outside.

57
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:02,719
From Juno, a spacecraft that has been
looping around Jupiter since 2016,

58
00:03:02,719 --> 00:03:06,980
weighing the planet with gravity and
listening to it in microwaves.

59
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:12,300
I'm two hundred kilometres down, and the
wind still hasn't stopped.

60
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:14,689
It won't for a long time.

61
00:03:15,049 --> 00:03:20,111
Juno found that Jupiter's famous stripes
are jet streams that reach about three

62
00:03:20,111 --> 00:03:21,713
thousand kilometres deep.

63
00:03:22,073 --> 00:03:27,334
And the Great Red Spot, a storm twice as
wide as Earth, has roots about five

64
00:03:27,334 --> 00:03:28,996
hundred kilometres down.

65
00:03:29,356 --> 00:03:34,219
Galileo's entire journey would fit inside
it almost three times.

66
00:03:35,719 --> 00:03:37,397
Pressure's climbing too fast.

67
00:03:37,757 --> 00:03:39,332
The hull is groaning.

68
00:03:39,692 --> 00:03:43,145
Wait, the outside looks thick and heavy,
like we're under water.

69
00:03:43,855 --> 00:03:45,058
There's no splash.

70
00:03:45,418 --> 00:03:46,396
No surface.

71
00:03:46,756 --> 00:03:51,620
Hydrogen, almost ninety percent of
Jupiter, is squeezed so hard that it

72
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:54,224
slowly turns from a gas into a liquid.

73
00:03:54,584 --> 00:03:56,287
Mara isn't falling anymore.

74
00:03:56,647 --> 00:03:57,539
She's sinking.

75
00:03:58,249 --> 00:03:59,582
I'm in an ocean.

76
00:03:59,942 --> 00:04:01,715
An ocean bigger than Earth.

77
00:04:02,425 --> 00:04:07,265
Roughly halfway to the centre, the
pressure gets so extreme that it rips

78
00:04:07,265 --> 00:04:09,484
electrons off the hydrogen atoms.

79
00:04:09,844 --> 00:04:13,555
The ocean starts to conduct electricity,
like liquid metal.

80
00:04:14,265 --> 00:04:17,206
Every compass on board is spinning.

81
00:04:17,566 --> 00:04:19,346
There's current everywhere.

82
00:04:20,056 --> 00:04:25,557
This metal ocean, churning inside a planet
that spins once every ten hours, is a

83
00:04:25,557 --> 00:04:26,451
giant dynamo.

84
00:04:26,811 --> 00:04:31,306
It powers a magnetic field up to
fifty-four times stronger than Earth's,

85
00:04:31,306 --> 00:04:35,864
the same field that traps the intense
radiation the real probe had to fly

86
00:04:35,864 --> 00:04:37,237
through on its way in.

87
00:04:38,737 --> 00:04:40,514
I've lost the link to the orbiter.

88
00:04:40,874 --> 00:04:42,108
Recording anyway.

89
00:04:42,818 --> 00:04:47,093
For decades, textbooks drew a small, solid
rock at Jupiter's centre.

90
00:04:47,453 --> 00:04:51,104
Juno's gravity measurements say that
picture is wrong.

91
00:04:51,464 --> 00:04:53,663
The core is huge, and fuzzy.

92
00:04:54,023 --> 00:04:56,488
Partly dissolved into the metal around it.

93
00:04:56,848 --> 00:05:00,985
In some models, it stretches more than
halfway to the cloud tops.

94
00:05:01,345 --> 00:05:06,656
One explanation: when Jupiter was young, a
planet about ten times the mass of Earth

95
00:05:06,656 --> 00:05:11,903
may have hit it head-on, shattering its
core so badly that it never settled again.

96
00:05:12,613 --> 00:05:16,609
There's no floor and no rock, it just gets
denser and hotter.

97
00:05:17,319 --> 00:05:22,639
Near the centre, scientists estimate the
temperature at around twenty-four thousand

98
00:05:22,639 --> 00:05:23,152
degrees.

99
00:05:23,512 --> 00:05:27,060
More than four times hotter than the
surface of the Sun.

100
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:30,336
This is Vesper, final log.

101
00:05:30,696 --> 00:05:33,345
If anyone ever hears this, it was
beautiful.

102
00:05:34,845 --> 00:05:36,351
Mara isn't real.

103
00:05:36,711 --> 00:05:39,354
But the probe that went first was.

104
00:05:39,714 --> 00:05:44,902
In 1995, a small machine fell into the
biggest planet in our solar system, and

105
00:05:44,902 --> 00:05:48,228
for fifty-eight minutes, it told us what
it found.

106
00:05:48,588 --> 00:05:52,697
Everything deeper, we've pieced together
since, from far outside.

107
00:05:53,057 --> 00:05:55,209
A place no one will ever see.

108
00:05:55,569 --> 00:05:56,832
But we know it's there.

109
00:05:57,192 --> 00:06:00,801
If you want to fall into another world
with us, subscribe.
